Step 1: Check Memory Usage

Command:

free -h

Example Output:

              total   used   free   shared  buff/cache  available
Mem:           8Gi    7.5Gi  200Mi   500Mi      300Mi       350Mi
Swap:          2Gi    1.8Gi  200Mi

Explanation:-
| Field      | Meaning                                                               |
| ---------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| total      | Total RAM installed                                                   |
| used       | RAM currently in use                                                  |
| free       | Completely unused RAM                                                 |
| buff/cache | RAM used for filesystem cache (Linux can free this if needed)         |
| available  | Memory available for starting new applications without heavy swapping |
| Swap       | Disk space used as extra memory when RAM is full                      |

What do you observe?
Total RAM = 8 GB
Used RAM = 7.5 GB
Free RAM = 200 MB
Swap usage = 1.8 GB

This indicates the system is under memory pressure because almost all RAM is used and swap is also heavily utilized.

Step 2: Identify the Process Using the Most Memory

Command:

ps -eo pid,user,%mem,%cpu,comm --sort=-%mem | head

Example Output:

PID    USER    %MEM   %CPU   COMMAND
2500   mysql    45.5   30.0   mysqld
3100   root     20.0   10.0   java
4500   nginx     5.0    2.0   nginx

Explanation

Here:

MySQL is using 45.5% of RAM.
Java is using 20% of RAM.

Now you know which applications are consuming the most memory.

Step 3: Check Running Processes

Command:

top

or

htop
Example:

PID   USER   RES     COMMAND
2500  mysql  3.5G    mysqld
3100  root   1.6G    java

This gives you a live view of memory usage.

Step 4: Check Swap Usage

Command:

swapon --show

Example Output:

NAME      TYPE  SIZE  USED
/swapfile file   2G   1.8G

Why is this important?

When RAM becomes full, Linux starts using swap, which is disk space acting as temporary memory.

Disk access is much slower than RAM, so high swap usage often causes poor performance.

Step 5: Check System Logs

Ubuntu

tail -50 /var/log/syslog

RHEL

tail -50 /var/log/messages

Look for:

Out of Memory (OOM) messages
Application crashes
Memory allocation failures

You can also search specifically:

dmesg | grep -i oom

or

journalctl -k | grep -i oom
                                              
